What you're deploying

Browser → saarathi-frontend.workers.dev   (React build, static)
             │ API calls
             ▼
          saarathi-backend.workers.dev    (Worker → your FastAPI container)
             │
             ▼
          Neon Postgres                   (external — Cloudflare has no Postgres)
Cost: Containers need the Workers Paid plan, $5/month — the free plan can't run them. Neon has a free tier.

Files I created
File	Purpose
backend/Dockerfile	Your app as an image. Migrates then starts, like make run
backend/.dockerignore	Keeps .env and .venv out of the image
backend/wrangler.jsonc	Container settings + non-secret env vars
backend/cloudflare/index.ts	The Worker that forwards requests to the container
backend/package.json	Deploy tooling only (wrangler). Not in the image
frontend/wrangler.jsonc	Static-site config with SPA fallback
I did not run any deploy commands — those touch your account, so they're yours to run.

Step 1 — Cloudflare account
Sign up at dash.cloudflare.com
Go to Workers & Pages → subscribe to the Paid plan ($5/mo)
Make sure Docker Desktop is running (wrangler uses it to build your image)
Step 2 — Database on Neon
Sign up at neon.tech, create a project (pick a region near your users)
Copy the connection string. It looks like:

postgresql://neondb_owner:npg_xxxx@ep-cool-name.ap-southeast-1.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require
Change postgresql:// to postgresql+psycopg:// — your app needs the driver named. Keep everything else. Save this; it's your DATABASE_URL.
Step 3 — Log in to Cloudflare from your terminal

cd ~/Desktop/POC_SARATHI/Latest_POC/saarathi-poc-new/backend
npm install
npx wrangler login
A browser opens — click Allow.

Step 4 — Set your secrets
Run each one; it prompts you to paste the value (nothing is written to disk):


npx wrangler secret put DATABASE_URL          # the postgresql+psycopg:// string from Step 2
npx wrangler secret put OPENROUTER_API_KEY    # copy from backend/.env
npx wrangler secret put SAARTHI_STATIC_TOKEN  # copy from backend/.env
npx wrangler secret put MITRA_ORIGIN_URL      # copy from backend/.env
Everything non-secret (API_PREFIX, MITRA_ENABLED, pool sizes…) is already in wrangler.jsonc, copied from your .env.

Step 5 — Deploy the backend

npx wrangler deploy
First run takes 5–15 minutes — it builds the image, pushes it to Cloudflare's registry, then creates the Worker. You'll get a URL:


https://saarathi-backend.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev
Copy that URL. Check it's alive:


curl https://saarathi-backend.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev/saarathi-service/healthz
# {"status":"ok"}
The first request wakes the container and runs your migrations against Neon, so it may take ~30s. If it fails, npx wrangler tail streams the logs live.

Step 6 — Deploy the frontend

cd ../frontend
Edit public/config.js — put your backend URL in, with the prefix and /api/:


window.__APP_CONFIG__ = {
  API_BASE_URL: 'https://saarathi-backend.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev/saarathi-service/api/',
};
Then:


npm install
npm run build
npx wrangler deploy
You'll get https://saarathi-frontend.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev. Copy it.

Step 7 — Let the frontend talk to the backend (CORS)
Right now the backend rejects the frontend's origin. In backend/wrangler.jsonc, replace the placeholder:


"FRONTEND_ORIGINS": "https://saarathi-frontend.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev",
Then redeploy the backend (fast this time — the image is unchanged):


cd ../backend && npx wrangler deploy
Step 8 — Re-apply your education guardrail
Your Neon database is brand new, so migration 0010 seeded the original "friendly General Support Assistant" prompt — your education prompt only exists in your local Postgres. Don't hand-edit the row this time (that's what caused the checksum mismatch). Instead:

In wrangler.jsonc, set "SAARTHI_ADMIN_ENABLED": "1" and redeploy
POST /saarathi-service/api/agents/general_support/config with the full spec including your education prompt — this recomputes the checksum, creates version 2, and reloads the registry
Set it back to "0" and redeploy
Tell me when you're at this step and I'll write the exact curl with your spec filled in.

Three things that will bite you
Mitra may reject the container. Mitra gates admission on the Origin header and possibly source IP. Your container's egress IP is Cloudflare's, not your office's — if Mitra allowlists IPs, interviews will fail while normal chat works fine. Worth checking with whoever runs Mitra before you debug it as a code problem.

max_instances is 1, on purpose. Don't raise it. Two instances means two Mitra WebSockets on one interview — the exact thing your CLAUDE.md forbids.

The container sleeps after 30 minutes idle. The next request cold-starts it (~20–30s) and in-memory Mitra channels are dropped. That's recoverable — re-authenticating with the same remote_session_id resumes the interview — but the first request after idle will feel slow. Lower sleepAfter to save money, raise it for responsiveness.